\begin{description}
\item[* name:] TArgReflSpelling1
\item[* relevant examples:] I gave myself a present, One shouldn't degrade 
oneself, the baby hurt itself/herself/himself
\item[* language:] (Dutch, English, Spanish) English
\item[* kind of rule:] (M-rule, S-rule, Transferrule) M-rule
\item[* subgrammar:] CLAUSEtoSENTENCE
\item[* class:] (name of rule, transformation or filter class) TC\_
ArgReflSpelling
\item[* date of creation:] 17-AUG-1988 11:38:19  
\item[* author:] margreet sanders
\item[* copied from:] Dutch TArgReflSpelling1 and English TObjReflInsertion1
\item[* modification:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item date: ../../..\ \ \ \ \ author:
\item date: ../../..\ \ \ \ \ author:
\end{enumerate}
\item[* task:] to spell out the second of two identical NPVARs as a reflexive. 
Even the LEVEL of the two VARs must be the same. The cases of the two VARs 
may differ, though.
\item[\ \ strategy:] (also: deviations from accepted proposals) It is assumed 
that the nearest (= rightmost) NPVAR in S is the antecedent for the reflexive.
\item[\ \ rejected alternatives:]
\item[\ \ clarification of variables:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the splitting up into subrules:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the collapsing of rules:] (e.g. clarification of parameters)
\item[\ \ differences analytical/generative version:]
\item[\  \ suggested semantics:]
\item[\ \ presuppositions:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item dictionary:\\

\item rules:\\
For the formulation of the third person singular rules (with reference to sex 
and gender) see TObjReflInsertion1: inanimate NPs have sexes = [], but may 
still have a gender specification (for cases like {\em ship\/}, which might be 
referred to as {\em she\/}. Animate NPs may have only one sex (even
words like {\em doctor\/} should have only one sex per entry); persproNPs have 
no sexes, but only gender.
\end{enumerate}
\item[\ \ consequences in the grammar:]
\item[\ \ translation aspects:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate} 
\item within this language:\\

\item in other languages:\\

\end{enumerate}
\item[* relevant literature/documents:]
\item[\ \ remarks:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item problems:\\
No condition has been formulated on the presence of reflexives in case of a 
reflexive verb, since argument reflexives and inherent reflexives may occur in 
one sentence: reveal yourself to yourself. Hence, false paths may be created 
here in analysis, making the wrong reflNP into an NPVAR. This will only be 
solved in TC\_ReflexiveSpelling, just preceding the pattern rules.
\item modifications:\\

\end{enumerate}
\end{description}
